@page "/learn/supply-parameter-from-session"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Session state · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Session state</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">A form takes two steps. Where do you keep what they typed in step 1?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Pages &amp; navigation · Static SSR · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>[SupplyParameterFromSession]</code> ties a property to the user's
        session. Session itself isn't new: in .NET 10 you read and wrote it by hand through <code>HttpContext</code>.
        The new attribute does the loading and saving for you.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a two-step restock order. Step 1 asks how many packs of each product you want. Step 2 shows the
        order and sends it to the supplier.
    </p>
    <p>
        The page is static SSR: the server remembers nothing between requests. The quick .NET 10 answer is to carry
        step 1's numbers into step 2 as hidden form fields. That's what this page does.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="Two steps, carried in hidden fields" Url="demo/session/restock/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: hidden fields" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Enter some quantities and press <b>Review order →</b>. Then press <b>⟳</b> above the frame. You're back
            on an empty step 1. Review again and press <b>Send with one hidden field edited to 9999</b>. That's
            what anyone can do in DevTools. It sends 9999 packs, past step 1's 99-pack limit.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The draft only lives in the page</h2>
    <p>Here's what step 2 sends back to the server:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Step 2's form, as the browser holds it</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;form method="post"&gt;
  &lt;input type="hidden" name="Draft[31]" value="7" /&gt;   <span class="dim">Gorgonzola</span>  <span class="err">editable in DevTools</span>
  &lt;input type="hidden" name="Draft[21]" value="12" /&gt;  <span class="dim">Scones</span>
  &lt;button&gt;Send to supplier&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The draft only exists in that page. A refresh, a link or Back loads the page fresh, so the draft is lost.
        And Send trusts whatever numbers the browser sends, so step 1's limit means nothing.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Keep the draft on the server</h2>
    <p>Turn on session once, as in .NET 10. Then put the same property on both steps, and assign to it.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// Program.cs, unchanged from .NET 10</span>
builder.Services.AddDistributedMemoryCache();
builder.Services.AddSession();
app.UseSession();

<span class="dim">// Step 1 and step 2</span>
<span class="hl">[SupplyParameterFromSession(Name = "restock-draft")]</span>
public Dictionary&lt;string, int&gt;? Draft { get; set; }

<span class="dim">// Step 1's handler. Keys must be strings, so product 31 goes in as "31"</span>
<span class="hl">Draft = packs.ToDictionary(p =&gt; $"{p.Key}", p =&gt; p.Value);</span>
Navigation.NavigateTo("demo/session/restock/review");</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The attribute loads the property from session when the page loads. It saves it when the request ends,
        even if you redirect. The data stays on the server. The browser only holds a cookie,
        <code>.AspNetCore.Session</code>, that says which session is theirs. One catch: session only takes simple
        types, and dictionaries only with string keys.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same order, kept in session" Url="demo/session/restock"
                Label=".NET 11: [SupplyParameterFromSession]" Side="after">
        <Caption>
            Enter some quantities and press <b>Review order →</b>. Press <b>⟳</b> above the frame, or
            <b>← Change quantities</b>. Your numbers are still there. Review again and press
            <b>Send to supplier</b>. The draft is gone, and step 1 is empty again.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two pages behind Fig. 2:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/SessionDraft/RestockLines.razor" Feature="supply-parameter-from-session">
        <Caption>
            Step 1 sets <code>Draft</code> in its handler, then redirects. Product ids go in as strings, because
            session dictionaries need string keys.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/SessionDraft/RestockReview.razor" Feature="supply-parameter-from-session">
        <Caption>
            Step 2 uses the same key. Sending sets <code>Draft</code> to <code>null</code>, which removes it from
            session.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Hidden fields, or session by hand</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 10 the attribute doesn't exist, so using it fails with CS0246. You had two choices instead. Use
        hidden fields, or read and write session yourself. Here's what each did in a .NET 10 build, plus what happens if you
        forget one line:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The two .NET 10 ways, and what a .NET 10 build did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// 1. hidden fields</span>
&lt;input type="hidden"
  name="Draft[31]" value="7" /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">a refresh empties step 1, and an edited value of 9999 goes through</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ sends 9999 packs of Gorgonzola Telino</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// 2. session by hand</span>
Http.Session.SetString("draft",
  JsonSerializer.Serialize(draft));</pre>
                <span class="why">you write save code in every form handler, and load code on every page</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ the next page finds the draft: {"31":7}</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// 2 again: the same handler,</span>
<span class="err">// minus the line that saves</span></pre>
                <span class="why">an easy mistake: it still redirects, with no error and no warning</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the next page says "There's no draft in session."</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Session by hand works, and still works in .NET 11. But every page repeats the same load and save code. Forget
        the save and the user's work is lost without a word. Here's that version:
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/SessionDraft/RestockLinesByHand.razor" Feature="supply-parameter-from-session">
        <Caption>
            Step 1 the .NET 10 way. It loads the draft from session and parses it when the page loads. Its
            <b>Save draft</b> button saves the draft back to session, then reloads the page, so the numbers you
            see come from session. Fig. 3 does the same loading and saving with one attribute.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One attribute loads and saves</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 there's no save line to forget. You declare the property and assign to it. It's loaded when
        the page loads and saved when the request ends, so a redirect still saves. Set it to
        <code>null</code> to remove it. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Simple types, string keys.</b> Strings, ints, bools, Guids, <code>DateTime</code>s and int enums, plus arrays, lists and dictionaries of those, with string keys. With a <code>Dictionary&lt;int, int&gt;</code>, a <code>decimal</code> or your own class, the page crashes with <code>InvalidOperationException</code> as soon as it loads. For a big draft, keep an id in session and the draft in your database.</li>
        <li><b>Static SSR pages only.</b> Interactive components have no HTTP request, so the property stays <code>null</code>.</li>
        <li><b>One session per browser.</b> Another browser or a private window gets its own. After the idle timeout (20 minutes by default, 30 here) the draft is gone.</li>
        <li><b>One server's memory.</b> <code>AddDistributedMemoryCache</code> empties on a restart. With several servers behind a load balancer, either send each user back to the same server (sticky sessions), or use a shared cache such as Redis.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
